Hello Pat,

I'm writing on behalf of our IT team, who are planning a network upgrade acro=
ss our branches
early next year. They've asked me to pass on some details and one question, s=
ince I'm the one
who deals with you on the kiosks.

What's happening. The council's IT contractor is replacing the routers and sw=
itches at all nine
of our branch libraries between January and March. Each branch will be done o=
n a single day,
usually a Monday when we open late. The contractor expects each branch to be =
without a network
for between two and six hours.

The kiosks. Seven of the nine branches have one of your kiosks at the entranc=
e. The other two are
small enough that staff sign visitors in by hand. At the busiest branch, Cent=
ral, the kiosk signs
in around 150 people a day, mostly volunteers, contractors and community grou=
p leaders. Reading
groups and the job club are the heaviest users.

Our worry. On the days the network is down, branches will still be open. Staf=
f will be busy
helping people whose library accounts won't work either, so they won't have t=
ime to run a paper
book as well. If the kiosk simply stops, visitors will walk past it and we'll=
 have no record of
who was in the building, which matters for fire safety.

What we'd do anyway. Each branch has a printed fire list of staff on duty. We=
 could also put a
paper sheet next to the kiosk on those days, but past experience says people =
ignore it when there
is a screen next to it.

Other details. Our IT team will send you the new network addresses once they =
have them, in
case anything on your side needs updating. They'd also like to know whether t=
he kiosks need any
particular ports or websites opened through the new firewall, so they can set=
 it up correctly
from the first day rather than troubleshoot afterwards.

Visitors on the day. The busiest groups at Central meet on Monday evenings, a=
fter the late
opening starts, so the outage there may overlap with them. The reading group =
alone can bring 25
people, most of whom sign in within ten minutes of each other. We'd rather no=
t ask them to queue
at the desk, and some of our regular volunteers find it awkward to sign a pap=
er sheet when they
are used to the screen. If the kiosk could keep working through the outage, i=
t would save the
branch managers a real headache.

Timing. The first branch, Eastgate, is due on Monday 11 January. We'd like to=
 know where we
stand well before then, so that branch managers can plan.

Training. If any change is needed in how staff use the kiosks during the outa=
ge, we'd want a short
note we can pass to branch managers. They're comfortable with the dashboard b=
ut not with anything
technical.

Contact. For anything technical, our IT lead would rather hear from your supp=
ort team directly
than through me, though I'm happy to stay copied.

So, the question our IT team most wants answered: will the kiosks keep signin=
g visitors in while
a branch is offline, and catch up once the connection is back?

